\paragraph{Implication (read literally).} With $d$ = cluster depth, $d/D\approx0.76$--$0.81$ gives $D\approx400$ Mpc $\ll r\approx14\,300$ Mpc ($D/r\approx0.03$): the CoE would be \emph{inside} our vision shell, at $z\approx0.1$.
This conflicts with a CoE beyond the horizon. Conversely, $D>r$ with $d=321$ Mpc forces $d/D<0.022$; then the $m=3$ term ($\propto d/D$) would be $\approx-0.0008$, not the measured $-0.029$,
and keeping $d/D\approx0.8$ with $D>r$ would need $d\gtrsim11$ Gpc, far beyond the clusters.
So either the CoE is at $D\approx0.4$ Gpc, or the fitted $d/D$ is an effective shape parameter (it sets the $m=3$/$m=2$ ratio) and is \emph{not} the cluster-depth-to-CoE-distance ratio; in that case $D$, $M(<D)$ and $L$ above are not physical values.
A single effective depth is used; the sample spans $z=0.003$--$0.45$, and a per-cluster-redshift calculation could shift $D$.

\paragraph{Caveats.}
(i) The tidal form is first order in $d/D$, and the solution has $d/D\approx0.8$.
The full numerical model (all orders) gives a positive in-plane dipole at $b_0=0^\circ$--$15^\circ$ and
re-optimized $R^2=0.776\ (0^\circ),\ 0.777\ (15^\circ),\ 0.752\ (33.5^\circ)$, so $|b_0|=33.5^\circ$ is an
artifact of truncation; $b_0=0^\circ$--$15^\circ$ is not resolved. Full model at $b_0=0^\circ$: $\kappa\approx0.45$--$0.46$, $d/D\approx0.76$, $D/L\approx7.6$, $\ell_0\approx14.4^\circ$;
at $b_0=15^\circ$: $\kappa\approx0.27$, $d/D\approx0.74$, $D/L\approx11.3$ ($d/D=0.79$ is the first-order analytic free fit, not the full model).
At $d/D=0.76$ the two-term series is poor near $\mu=-1$ (point mass: exact $21.5$ vs series $4.3$ in units of $k/D^3$).
(ii) The coefficients $\tfrac92,\tfrac{15}{2}$ are point-mass values. (iii) The 71 sector points overlap and are not independent.
